🚽 Test case: round-20 review finding #3 -- semantic_analyze_with_scope_
🚽 tracking()'s switch had no case for NODE_ERROR_STATEMENT (`baka(...)`),
🚽 falling to the generic `default:` (does nothing), so a native call
🚽 nested inside baka's argument never reached semantic_check_native_
🚽 call() at all: `yapping`'s own fixed-format-argument type checking
🚽 (its parameter 0 is declared STDROT_STRING) never ran. This is
🚽 deliberately a WRONGLY-typed nested call (42, an int, where yapping's
🚽 format parameter requires a string) rather than a merely-valid one --
🚽 a valid nested call would still "pass" even with the traversal gap
🚽 completely unfixed, proving nothing about whether semantic checking
🚽 actually descended into baka's argument. Now caught statically, via
🚽 the normal native-call argument-type checking every other call
🚽 position already gets.
skibidi main {
    baka(yapping(42));
    bussin 0;
}
